const String pageAuth = r'''# Authentication and authorization

Guards are registered in `bootstrap/app.dart`:

```dart
.withAuth((auth) {
  auth.token<User>('token', ModelUserProvider<User>(User.definition));
  auth.jwt<User>('jwt', ModelUserProvider<User>(User.definition));
})
```

The model mixes in `AuthenticatableModel`. `Auth.user()`, `Auth.id()`,
`Auth.check()` and `Auth.guard('jwt')` work anywhere inside a request, job or
command; the `auth` (or `auth:jwt`) middleware answers 401 with
`{"message": "Unauthenticated."}`.

## Personal access tokens (Sanctum)

```dart
final user = await Auth.retrieveByCredentials<User>({
  'email': email,
  'password': password,
});
if (user == null) {
  throw ValidationException.withMessages({'email': ['Invalid credentials.']});
}
final token = await Auth.issueToken(user, 'cli', abilities: ['posts:read']);
return {'token': token.plainTextToken, 'token_type': 'Bearer'};
```

Tokens use Sanctum 4's format and table: the client receives
`{id}|{40 random characters}{crc32b checksum}`, the `personal_access_tokens`
row stores the SHA-256 of everything after the `|`, and `tokenable_type` is
the model's Laravel class (`App\Models\User`). A token created by Laravel
authenticates in Daravel and the other way round. `SANCTUM_TOKEN_PREFIX`
adds Sanctum's `token_prefix`; `AUTH_TOKEN_EXPIRATION` (minutes) sets a
global lifetime. `tokenCan`, `revokeCurrent`, `revokeAll` and
`sanctum:prune-expired` are available on `Auth.tokenGuard()`.

The token's `{id}` is the table's key: an integer, as Sanctum's migration
makes it. When the Laravel side gives its `PersonalAccessToken` model
`HasUuids` or `HasUlids`, register the guard with the same key, so new tokens
get a UUID or ULID and only such ids are looked up:
`auth.token<User>('token', provider, keyType: KeyType.uuid)`. A token whose
id cannot be the table's key is refused before the database is asked.

When no account matches the e-mail, the password is still hashed against a
dummy hash, so a failed login takes the same time whether or not the account
exists.

## JWT

`Auth.issueJwt(user)` signs HS256 tokens with `JWT_SECRET` (or `APP_KEY`),
or EdDSA with `JWT_PUBLIC_KEY` / `JWT_PRIVATE_KEY`. Verification rejects
`alg: none`, algorithms without a configured key, bad signatures and expired
tokens. `Auth.jwtGuard().invalidate()` revokes a token until it expires (plus
the leeway): the denylist lives in the default cache store, so with
`CACHE_STORE=database` (or Redis) every machine refuses the token. When
the cache store is in memory (`memory` or `process`), the revoked ids get
a store of their own on the coordinator, so every worker isolate refuses
the token and keys the application caches cannot evict it. That store
keeps `JWT_DENYLIST_MAX_ENTRIES` ids (100 000): expired ones go first,
and if a live one must go, the server logs a warning once. Size it from
the token lifetime times the logouts per second, or use `database` or
Redis.

A Laravel application on tymon/jwt-auth (its `jwt` guard with
`lock_subject`) shares its tokens with Daravel when both use the same
secret and these settings:

```dotenv
JWT_SECRET=<the Laravel JWT_SECRET>
JWT_SUBJECT_CLASS=App\Models\User   # prv = sha1 of this class name, as tymon writes it
JWT_ISSUER_CLAIM=https://api.example.com   # iss for issued tokens (default APP_URL); tymon requires it, checks nothing
JWT_TTL=23040                       # minutes, tymon's ttl
JWT_LEEWAY=0                        # seconds, tymon's leeway
```

A token Laravel issued then signs the same user in on Daravel and the
other way round; a `prv` for another model class is refused.

## Hashing

`Hash.make` / `Hash.check` run in compute isolates. Drivers: `bcrypt`
(default, cost 12, `$2y$` like Laravel), `argon2id` and `pbkdf2`.
`Hash.check` detects the driver from the hash. With `HASH_VERIFY=true` (the
default) it refuses foreign formats except the strong family (bcrypt and
argon2id), so switching `HASH_DRIVER` between those two keeps every
existing password valid while `Hash.needsRehash` and
`rehashPasswordIfRequired` upgrade hashes at login. Keep bcrypt while a
Laravel app shares the users table: Laravel verifies only bcrypt by default.

At most `HASH_CONCURRENCY` hashes (default: one per processor) run at once,
each in a warm isolate that is reused, so a burst of logins waits its turn
instead of starting an isolate per attempt (with argon2id at 64 MiB each,
enough to run out of memory). Up to `HASH_QUEUE` hashes (1024; `0` for no
limit) wait, each for at most `HASH_QUEUE_TIMEOUT` (`30s`); past either
`Hash.make` and `Hash.check` throw `HashingSaturatedException`, answered as
`503` with `Retry-After: 1`.

On Linux, bcrypt runs in the system's C library (libxcrypt's `crypt_rn`,
the implementation PHP uses), so a login costs what it costs in Laravel; on
other systems, or with `DARAVEL_NATIVE_CRYPT=false`, a Dart implementation
gives the same hashes about 1.5 times slower. Both read `$2a$`, `$2b$` and
`$2y$` hashes and use the first 72 bytes of a password, as PHP does.

## Encryption

`Crypt.encryptString` / `decryptString` produce Laravel's payload
(`base64(json{iv, value, mac, tag})`) keyed by `APP_KEY`. The cipher is
`APP_CIPHER`: `AES-256-CBC` (the default, like Laravel; encrypt-then-MAC
with HMAC-SHA256) or `AES-256-GCM`. Use the same value as any Laravel app
you share data with.
`APP_PREVIOUS_KEYS` enables key rotation. `Crypt.encrypt` carries values
as PHP `serialize()` text, as Laravel's `Crypt::encrypt` does: a map or
list encrypted here is an array in Laravel and the other way round (a PHP
object arrives as `PhpObject`). Payloads made by Daravel 1.17 or earlier,
which held JSON, still decrypt.

## Authorization

```dart
.withPolicies((gate) {
  gate.define('publish', (user, args) => user is User && user.isEditor);
  gate.policy<Post>(const PostPolicy());
})
```

`Gate.allows`, `Gate.denies`, `Gate.authorize` (403 with `This action is
unauthorized.`) and the `can:update,post` middleware work like Laravel's.
Policy methods default to `false`.

## Laravel equivalents

More of Laravel that this works with, and the API to look up on pub.dev.

| Laravel | Daravel | API | Note |
| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |
| `$user->currentAccessToken()->delete()` | `await Auth.tokenGuard().revokeCurrent()` | `TokenGuard.revokeCurrent` |  |
| `$user->tokenCan('posts:read')` | `await Auth.tokenGuard().tokenCan('posts:read')` | `TokenGuard.tokenCan` |  |
''';
